---
/**
 * The notification act - phases.md 8.5. Gate A approved 2026-09-04.
 *
 * **It is now the last act on the page, not the third.** The owner swapped it with the
 * playlist act on 2026-09-04: the page reads better ending on the notification, so the
 * final CTA came with the swap, since the CTA belongs to whatever section closes the page.
 * Three things follow, and each of them is why this file differs from the 8.5 that was
 * approved - the copy moved off the bottom of the frame and the phone below it, the
 * section gained a scrubbed close, and it no longer inherits act two's device position.
 *
 * **The phone is a fixed object; the words take turns above it** (owner, Gate C rev 2).
 * The device stands at `--device-top` with its top ~57% in frame and does not move or
 * change size at any point in the section - the owner rejected the receding, shrinking
 * phone the first close used. Everything else therefore lives in the band between the
 * header and the phone: the claim occupies it, leaves on `--close-scene`, and the final
 * CTA rises into it on `--close-cta`.
 *
 * **One idea: the phone is asleep, and then it isn't.** The device is on screen at exactly
 * the size act two left it, its screen dark, the amber bloom at its lowest. It holds there
 * through a beat that buys nothing. Then the notification arrives: the screen comes up and
 * the bloom swells *with* it, as if cast by the phone, and the copy lands on the frame the
 * banner settles.
 *
 * **The bloom is a light source here, not atmosphere** - the one thing that makes this
 * section different from every other act. Everywhere else on the page a bloom sits behind
 * the content as weather; here it is driven by the same signal as the screen coming up, so
 * it reads as the room being lit by the phone. That is the idea the owner approved.
 *
 * **This is a second `<Device>`, not act two's carried across the boundary** (Gate B,
 * flagged and approved). `design-system.md` §6 asks for one persistent element "where
 * possible"; genuinely sharing one would mean restructuring both sections' pin
 * architecture, and the hero↔act-one seam already cost three rounds of Gate C. It used to
 * be pinned at act two's exit *geometry* so that the cut between the two was invisible -
 * that continuity is gone with the reorder, because the playlist act now sits between them
 * and there is nothing on screen to be continuous with. What survives is the **size**:
 * `--device-end-h` scaled by `--device-settle-scale`, shared tokens rather than numbers
 * written down twice, so it still reads as the same phone act two grew. **There is no
 * growth phase at all**: this act is short and about one event, not about the device.
 *
 * **Amber does not close the page; magenta does.** The atmosphere carries two fields that
 * cross-fade on the close - the amber room the phone lights, and the magenta
 * `design-system.md` §2 reserves for "Hero, CTA" - so the site still ends on the light it
 * opened with even though the act that ends it is no longer the magenta one.
 *
 * **The held beat is the owner's, and is not to be trimmed.** `.travel.sleep` drives
 * nothing whatsoever. It was flagged at Gate A as the first thing that would be cut for
 * pace and the owner kept it explicitly: it is the section's proof that the product is
 * calm. Shortening it is a design change and needs its own gate.
 *
 * **Amber bloom** (`design-system.md` §2 - "warmth, arrival"), reassigned to this act from
 * the old auto-add act by the owner: a notification *is* an arrival.
 *
 * References (`review-gates.md` §7): **revolut.com** for device continuity and for landing
 * the claim on the frame the product state resolves; **gsap.com** for the pinned, scrubbed
 * act; **superlist.com** for the restraint of moving exactly one thing per beat. The idea
 * itself is an argued divergence - none of the three animates a push notification -
 * recorded as such in `reference-study.md` §8 rather than back-filled with a citation.
 *
 * No tier or price appears anywhere in this section: notifications are not paywalled and
 * ship in the beta (owner, 2026-09-04).
 *
 * **8.8 landed.** The waitlist form, its five states and the App Store badge live on
 * `/waitlist`, a route the owner chose over a closing band here (Gate A, 2026-09-07). This
 * button is now a navigation to it rather than the in-page anchor it was, which is the only
 * thing 8.8 changed in this section - the approved composition is untouched.
 */
import BloomField from '../BloomField.astro';
import Button from '../primitives/Button.astro';
import Device from '../device/Device.astro';
import LockScreen from '../device/LockScreen.astro';
import { catalogue } from '../../i18n';

const t = catalogue(Astro.currentLocale);
---

<section id="notify" class="notify" data-notify-stage>
  <div class="atmosphere" data-notify-atmosphere>
    <div class="room"><BloomField act="notify" intensity="feature" /></div>
    {
      /*
      The second field is the page's closing light, and it is a *different* palette rather
      than a brightening of the first. Amber is the phone lighting the room and belongs to
      the notification; magenta is what `design-system.md` §2 assigns to "Hero, CTA", and
      the site is meant to close on the light it opened with. So the two cross-fade on
      `--notify-close`: the room goes out as the ask comes up. Both are radial gradients
      with no blur behind them, so carrying two fields costs three more composited layers
      and no paint work - measured, per `BloomField.astro`'s own note.

      Each sits in a wrapper this file owns rather than taking a `class` prop, because the
      masks and the cross-fade are this section's business and not the field's, and because
      a scoped rule written here is guaranteed to select an element written here.
    */
    }
    <div class="send-off"><BloomField act="cta" intensity="feature" /></div>
  </div>

  <div class="pin" data-notify-pin>
    {
      /*
      The light the phone throws into the room.
      
      Separate from `BloomField` on purpose. The bloom field lays out three drifting orbs to
      its own composition, which is right for an atmosphere but cannot express *this*: the
      first build drove the field's opacity from the wake and got a large amber orb sitting
      off to the left of the phone and a magenta one to the right - two coloured blobs that
      happened to brighten, rather than a room being lit by a screen. Cast light has to come
      from where the light source is, so it is its own radial, centred on the device, and the
      field stays behind it as a low ambient bed.
    */
    }
    <div class="cast" data-notify-cast></div>

    <div class="copy" data-notify-copy>
      <h2 class="headline">{t.home.notify.headline}</h2>
      <p class="sub">{t.home.notify.sub}</p>
    </div>

    <div class="device-slot">
      {/* Below `md` only: the cast light, carried by the phone because the phone moves. */}
      <div class="halo"></div>
      <Device>
        <LockScreen />
      </Device>
    </div>

    {
      /*
      Below `md` only: the distance between the phone's top and the copy's in flow, which is
      what decides how early the copy comes up over it. `notify.ts` reads its position to
      find where the phone would be unpinned. The class name avoids `notify`/`travel` for the
      scoping reason noted below.
    */
    }
    <div class="hold" data-notify-hold></div>

    <div class="cta" data-notify-cta inert>
      <h2 class="cta-headline">{t.home.claim}</h2>
      <p class="cta-sub">{t.home.notify.ctaSub}</p>
      <Button href="/waitlist">{t.home.notify.ctaAction}</Button>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!--
    Deliberately `sleep`/`wake`/`read`/`close`/`rest` and not `travel notify` - a modifier
    class that collides with the section's own class is selected by the section's own rule,
    because Astro scopes every element in a component with one shared `astro-cid`.
    `Feed.astro` and `Hero.astro` both carry this warning and the bug that earned it.
  -->
  <div class="travel sleep" data-notify-sleep></div>
  <div class="travel wake" data-notify-wake-track></div>
  <div class="travel read" data-notify-read></div>
  <div class="travel close" data-notify-close-track></div>
  <div class="travel rest"></div>
</section>

<style>
  /* How far the screen has come up, 0 asleep to 1 awake. Also drives the bloom. */
  @property --notify-wake {
    syntax: '<number>';
    inherits: true;
    initial-value: 0;
  }

  /*
   * The banner's arrival, 0 (not there) to 1 (settled). A separate property from
   * `--notify-wake` because the two are driven completely differently: the wake is scrubbed
   * and continuous, the arrival is a tween that runs to completion. See `notify.ts`.
   */
  @property --notify-banner {
    syntax: '<number>';
    inherits: true;
    initial-value: 0;
  }

  /*
   * The close, 0 (the notification standing) to 1 (the CTA up). Scrubbed, unlike the other
   * two: it is a change of composition rather than an event, and a composition change is
   * exactly the thing a visitor should be able to hold halfway. Act four's own rule,
   * inherited when the CTA moved here (owner, 2026-09-04).
   */
  @property --notify-close {
    syntax: '<number>';
    inherits: true;
    initial-value: 0;
  }

  .notify {
    position: relative;
    /* Establishes a block formatting context so the travel spacers translate into real
     * scrollable distance for the sticky pin, exactly as in `Hero.astro` and `Feed.astro`. */
    display: flow-root;
    /* Never `overflow: hidden` - it establishes a scroll container and silently breaks the
     * sticky pin. `clip` does not. */
    overflow-x: clip;
    background: var(--color-ink-950);

    /*
     * Act two's exit *size*, read from the shared tokens rather than restated. Only the
     * size survives the reorder: the phone used to be pinned at act two's exit position as
     * well, so the cut between the two sections was invisible, but act three now sits after
     * the playlist rather than immediately after act two and there is nothing left to be
     * continuous with. Keeping the size is what makes it the same phone.
     */
    --end-h: var(--device-end-h);
    --settle-scale: var(--device-settle-scale);
    /* The phone's own rendered height - the slot is now sized directly rather than being
     * `--end-h` tall and scaled inside its own box, because it sits in flow under the copy. */
    --device-h: calc(var(--end-h) * var(--settle-scale));
    /*
     * Where the phone stands, and everything else is measured from it (owner, Gate C rev 2).
     * The device sits low enough that only its top ~57% is in frame - 70svh of phone with
     * its top edge at 60svh - and it **never moves or resizes again**, at any point in the
     * section. That is the owner's instruction and it is the constraint the rest of this
     * file answers to: the frame above the phone is the only room there is, so the copy and
     * the final CTA both live in it and take turns.
     */
    --device-top: 60svh;
    /*
     * Both are centred in the band the header leaves above the phone - ~92px of header at
     * 1440 and ~76px at 390 - which works out to the same two numbers at both widths, so
     * neither needs a narrow override. The copy is 162px at 1440 and 148px at 390; the CTA
     * is 234px and 218px. Recomputing them is a matter of re-centring in
     * `--device-top` minus the header, not of taste.
     */
    --copy-top: 26svh;
    --cta-top: 22svh;
    /*
     * **Superseded on desktop at 8.14** (owner: the copy sat "a bit too high up", and the ask
     * should come down with it). Centring each block in the band left 130px or more of empty
     * frame between the words and the phone on a typical laptop window. Both now stand on the
     * phone instead - their bottom edge `--ask-gap` above `--device-top` - so the gap is one
     * ordinary margin at every height. The two `top`s above survive only as the mobile
     * block's reset values.
     */
    --ask-gap: var(--spacing-12);
    /*
     * The close swaps one for the other in that band, and the two cannot share one curve -
     * a crossfade in place would put the ask on top of the claim for the middle third of
     * the track. The scene goes quiet over the first 55% and the ask rises over the last
     * 55%, overlapping by 10%: enough to read as one movement, not enough for two pieces of
     * type to be legible in the same place at once.
     *
     * Named `--close-scene` and not `--close-device` since Gate C rev 2: the device is no
     * longer one of the things it moves. What it takes out is the copy and the phone's cast
     * light, which is the room going quiet before the ask.
     */
    --close-scene: clamp(0, calc(var(--notify-close) / 0.55), 1);
    --close-cta: clamp(0, calc((var(--notify-close) - 0.45) / 0.55), 1);
  }

  /*
   * Two stacked sticky layers with a negative margin, inside a very tall section - the
   * arrangement `Feed.astro` documents, including the forced compositing layers that
   * defend against the stray-dark-rectangle artifact reported on hardware-accelerated
   * rendering over a tall sticky ancestor.
   */
  /*
   * The overlap is a negative `margin-top` on `.pin`, not a negative `margin-bottom`
   * here - `Feed.astro` carries the full account and the measurement. In short: a
   * negative bottom margin on a sticky element extends its own constraint rectangle, so
   * the layer stays stuck a full viewport past the pin it is supposed to travel with.
   * Act two shipped that bug and the owner caught it at 8.5's Gate C; this section had
   * copied the same arrangement and would have done the same thing at the page's foot.
   */
  .atmosphere {
    position: sticky;
    top: 0;
    height: 100svh;
    pointer-events: none;

    /*
     * No opacity of its own any more: the layer now carries two fields at two very
     * different strengths, and one shared opacity here would have to be the louder of them.
     * Each field owns its own instead.
     */
    isolation: isolate;
    contain: paint;
    transform: translateZ(0);
  }

  /*
   * The room's ambient bed. Constant, and faint - every bit of *change* while the phone is
   * on screen comes from `.cast`, which is centred on the device. Driving this from the
   * wake as well was the second wrong turn in 8.5: the field lays its orbs out to its own
   * composition, so brightening it lit an amber patch to the left of the device and a
   * magenta one to the right, pulling the eye away from the one place the light is supposed
   * to be coming from.
   *
   * Feathered into the ground - superlist's move, as on every other act. But **wider and
   * pushed outward here**, and for a reason specific to this section: the bloom is the light
   * the phone casts, so it has to be visible in the room *around* the device. The first pass
   * reused act two's tighter mask, centred at 50% 54% - exactly where the phone stands - so
   * the entire bloom rendered behind the device and the section's whole idea was invisible
   * on screen. The hole in the middle is the phone's own footprint; what reads is the spill
   * past its edges.
   */
  .room {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    opacity: calc(0.1 * (1 - var(--notify-close)));
    -webkit-mask-image: radial-gradient(96% 78% at 50% 58%, #000 0%, #000 26%, transparent 82%);
    mask-image: radial-gradient(96% 78% at 50% 58%, #000 0%, #000 26%, transparent 82%);
  }

  /*
   * The strongest bloom moment on the page, and the last thing on it. It starts at a real
   * but low value rather than at nothing - `design-system.md` §5 keeps blooms present
   * rather than switching them on.
   *
   * Its mask sits **high** since Gate C rev 2, tracking the CTA rather than the foot of the
   * frame: the ask now rises into the band above the phone, and a send-off centred at 62%
   * would have put the page's brightest light behind the device instead of behind the words
   * it is lighting. It also stops well short of the bottom edge, which is the same call the
   * playlist act's atmosphere makes and for the same reason - a light that leaves the frame
   * through a straight edge stops reading as a light.
   */
  .send-off {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    opacity: calc(0.06 + var(--notify-close) * 0.79);
    /*
     * Centred on the ask, which since 8.14 stands on the phone rather than at `--cta-top`
     * (owner: the light comes down with the words). The ask's top is `--device-top` less
     * the gap and its own ~13rem, and the light's core sat ~4svh below that top before, so
     * it keeps that relation.
     */
    --send-off-y: calc(var(--device-top) - var(--ask-gap) - 13rem + 4svh);
    -webkit-mask-image: radial-gradient(
      118% 52% at 50% var(--send-off-y),
      #000 0%,
      #000 26%,
      transparent 94%
    );
    mask-image: radial-gradient(
      118% 52% at 50% var(--send-off-y),
      #000 0%,
      #000 26%,
      transparent 94%
    );
  }

  .pin {
    position: sticky;
    top: 0;
    height: 100svh;
    /* Pulls this layer back over `.atmosphere`, which keeps its own 100svh of flow. */
    margin-top: -100svh;
    z-index: 1;
    overflow: hidden;
    isolation: isolate;
    contain: paint;
    transform: translateZ(0);
  }

  /*
   * The scroll budgets, in order.
   *
   * `sleep` is the held beat and drives nothing at all - `notify.ts` never reads it. It
   * exists purely as scroll the visitor spends on a phone that does not move. `wake` and
   * `close` are the two driven phases. `read` sits between them: the notification seen
   * standing, before the page starts asking for anything. `rest` is the trailing hold, so
   * the page's last frame is seen standing rather than arriving and leaving.
   *
   * 236svh of travel against a 100svh pin - 2124px at 1440x900, against act two's 2243px,
   * so the page's longest section is still act two.
   */
  .travel.sleep {
    height: 46svh;
  }

  .travel.wake {
    height: 62svh;
  }

  .travel.read {
    height: 42svh;
  }

  .travel.close {
    height: 56svh;
  }

  .travel.rest {
    height: 30svh;
  }

  /* Desktop has neither: see the mobile block at the end of this file. */
  .halo,
  .hold {
    display: none;
  }

  /*
   * Centred on the phone rather than on the frame: the device stands at the foot of the
   * pin, and light that is supposed to be cast *by* it cannot be centred anywhere else. It
   * follows the phone down to `--device-top` plus a little, so its core sits on the part of
   * the screen that is actually lit rather than above it.
   *
   * It goes out on `--close-scene`. The phone no longer leaves - it stands there, lit, to
   * the end of the page - but its *room* does, because the amber cast is what would
   * otherwise still be washing up into the band the ask rises into.
   */
  .cast {
    position: absolute;
    left: 50%;
    top: 78%;
    width: min(170vw, 1500px);
    aspect-ratio: 1;
    transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
    pointer-events: none;
    z-index: 0;
    background: radial-gradient(
      circle closest-side,
      color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-bloom-amber) 88%, transparent) 0%,
      color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-bloom-amber) 30%, transparent) 34%,
      transparent 68%
    );
    /* The screen coming up and the room lighting are one signal - that is the whole idea
     * of the section, and it is why this reads the wake rather than scroll position. */
    opacity: calc(var(--notify-wake) * 0.55 * (1 - var(--close-scene)));
  }

  /*
   * The phone stands at the foot of the frame and does not move again (owner, Gate C rev 2).
   *
   * It was previously higher and receded at the close - up and scaled to 0.44 - so that the
   * ask could rise into the lower half. The owner rejected the resize outright: a phone
   * that shrinks is a picture of a phone, and the size is the one thing tying this device
   * to act two's. So it keeps act two's rendered size *and* keeps it for the whole section,
   * and the composition moved around it instead: `--device-top` at 60svh leaves the top
   * ~57% of the phone in frame, cropped by the pin's bottom edge, with the clock and the
   * notification - the only two things on it a visitor has to read - well inside the crop.
   *
   * There is therefore no close transform here at all, and nothing above may assume the
   * phone gets out of the way. Everything else in this pin lives in the band above
   * `--device-top`; `notify.spec.ts` asserts both halves of that - the CTA clears the
   * phone's top edge across the whole close, and the phone's rendered height never changes.
   */
  .device-slot {
    position: absolute;
    left: 50%;
    top: var(--device-top);
    height: var(--device-h);
    aspect-ratio: 390 / 844;
    transform: translateX(-50%);
    pointer-events: none;
    z-index: 1;
  }

  /*
   * The copy sits above the phone and is the section's claim. It arrives with the banner -
   * one signal, so the claim and its proof cannot drift apart (act two's rule, and
   * revolut.com's).
   *
   * It now *leaves* at the close rather than dimming and staying. With the phone standing
   * still, the band above it is the only room in the pin and the ask needs all of it, so
   * the two take turns: the claim goes out on `--close-scene`, the ask comes up on
   * `--close-cta`. The reduced-motion branch at the bottom of this file is what keeps that
   * from costing an RM visitor the section's words - it composes claim, ask and phone as
   * one static frame instead.
   */
  .copy {
    position: absolute;
    z-index: 2;
    left: 50%;
    bottom: calc(100% - var(--device-top) + var(--ask-gap));
    transform: translateX(-50%) translateY(calc((1 - var(--notify-banner)) * 12px));
    width: min(100% - 2 * var(--gutter), var(--content-max));
    text-align: center;
    opacity: calc(var(--notify-banner) * (1 - var(--close-scene)));
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--spacing-3);
    /*
     * Its opacity animates under a blurred `text-shadow`, which without a layer of its own
     * is re-rastered on every frame of it - act two's copy measured 460ms of raster against
     * 8ms promoted (`Feed.astro`). Promoted for the same reason.
     */
    will-change: transform, opacity;
  }

  .headline {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: var(--text-2xl);
    font-weight: 600;
    line-height: var(--leading-display);
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-display);
    color: var(--color-white);
    text-wrap: balance;
    max-width: var(--measure-display);

    /*
     * The copy overlaps the lit phone at narrow widths, so it carries act two's outro
     * legibility shadow (Gate C rev 2 there) - the full three layers since 8.14 (owner: the
     * single soft shadow did not read). Static, never animated.
     */
    text-shadow:
      0 1px 2px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.85),
      0 4px 20px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.75),
      0 0 48px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.65);
  }

  .sub {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: var(--text-body);
    line-height: var(--leading-body);
    color: var(--color-mute-200);
    /*
     * `--measure-tight` (15.8em) broke this into four short lines under a two-line headline
     * that is much wider than them, which read as a narrow column hanging off a wide one.
     * A touch wider gives two or three lines that sit under the headline's own width.
     */
    max-width: 26em;
    text-shadow:
      0 1px 2px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.85),
      0 4px 20px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.75),
      0 0 48px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.65);
  }

  /*
   * The close. It rises into the band the claim has just left - above the phone, never
   * across it. The phone is a fixed object in the lower half of every frame in this
   * section, so there is nowhere else for it to go, and that is the point: the ask is made
   * over a lit phone that is still showing the notification, not over an empty screen.
   */
  .cta {
    position: absolute;
    z-index: 3;
    inset-inline: 0;
    bottom: calc(100% - var(--device-top) + var(--ask-gap));
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--spacing-4);
    padding-inline: var(--gutter);
    text-align: center;
    opacity: var(--close-cta);
    transform: translateY(calc((1 - var(--close-cta)) * 44px));
  }

  /*
   * An `<a>` at opacity 0 is still clickable and still in the tab order, so the CTA ships
   * with `inert` in the HTML and `notify.ts` removes it once the close has actually begun.
   * It has to be an attribute rather than CSS: `pointer-events` would leave the link
   * focusable, and `visibility` cannot be driven from a number the way opacity can. The
   * reduced-motion branch, which never scrubs anything, clears it immediately.
   *
   * With JavaScript off this CTA stays inert, which is correct rather than a regression: it
   * also stays at opacity 0, since every scrubbed signal on this route sits at its
   * `@property` initial value. `Header.astro`'s "Join the waitlist" is the conversion path
   * that survives with no script at all, and it is fixed to the viewport throughout.
   */
  .cta[inert] {
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  .cta-headline {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: var(--text-3xl);
    font-weight: 700;
    line-height: var(--leading-display);
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-display);
    color: var(--color-white);
    text-wrap: balance;
    max-width: var(--measure-display);
  }

  .cta-sub {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: var(--text-lg);
    line-height: var(--leading-body);
    color: var(--color-mute-200);
    /* Twice `--measure-tight` since 8.14 (owner: "too narrow" - it ran to three short lines
     * under a one-line headline). */
    max-width: calc(2 * var(--measure-tight));
    /* At the wider measure the last word fell to a line of its own at 1691px. */
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  /*
   * Narrow needs no geometry of its own any more. The phone is the same 70svh of height at
   * both widths, the header's no-go band is ~10svh at 1440 and ~9svh at 390, and the copy
   * and CTA scale with the type - so centring each in the band above `--device-top` lands
   * on the same two svh values at both. The previous per-width `top`s were compensating for
   * a composition that has since been rebuilt around the phone.
   */

  /*
   * Reduced motion is a designed frame, not a disabled section (`design-system.md` §5).
   * `notify.ts` rests it on `--notify-wake: 1`, `--notify-banner: 1` and
   * `--notify-close: 1` - the screen up, the notification there, the CTA up - and the one
   * thing that must not carry over from the full build is the dim on the copy and the
   * phone, because the act's whole argument would then be something the visitor is asked to
   * squint at. Here the two simply coexist at full strength. The device keeps its close
   * transform: without it the phone would sit on top of the CTA rather than above it.
   */
  /* At `md` and up only since 8.14c round 8 (owner): below it, phones keep the full
   * motion build under Reduce Motion. */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) and (min-width: 768px) {
    /*
     * The claim and the ask cannot take turns in a frame that never moves, so reduced
     * motion gets its own arrangement of the same three things rather than a snapshot of
     * the last one: the claim at the top, the ask under it, the phone standing at the foot
     * with the notification on it. It is the only frame an RM visitor sees, and it is now a
     * more complete one than the scrubbed close - which is what `design-system.md` §5 means
     * by a designed state rather than a disabled one.
     *
     * These two `top`s are the one place in this file that is not derived from
     * `--device-top`; they are the band above it, split. Measured clearance to the phone's
     * top edge is 27px at 1440 and 27px at 390.
     */
    .copy {
      top: 11svh;
      bottom: auto;
      opacity: var(--notify-banner);
    }

    .cta {
      top: 31svh;
      bottom: auto;
    }
  }

  /*
   * Below `md`: only the phone is pinned (owner, 8.14c Gate C rounds 1 and 2). `.pin` stops
   * being the sticky frame and becomes an ordinary tall column, and the phone is the sticky
   * element. The pinned ask is removed - `Closing.astro` is its own section after this one.
   *
   * **The sequence** (round 3): the phone scrolls in first, and the copy enters the screen
   * behind it once 40% of the phone is in view - before it is halfway. The phone pins once 70%
   * of it is on screen and the copy keeps rising *over* it, never pinned itself. The screen
   * wakes while the copy crosses, and the notification lands at the moment the copy's bottom
   * reaches the phone's top edge (`notify.ts`); the phone lets go once the copy has cleared
   * that edge by `--release-gap`, and the two leave together. The section ends 60% of the
   * way down the phone, cutting it there.
   *
   * **How the release is timed, with no JavaScript.** A sticky element stays stuck until its
   * *margin* box reaches the end of its containing block. The copy is the last thing in
   * `.pin`, so the end of the block is the copy's bottom edge; a negative bottom margin of the
   * phone's height plus the gap puts the phone's margin-box bottom exactly `--release-gap`
   * above its own top edge, which is therefore where the copy's bottom sits when it lets go.
   * The same margin pulls the `.hold` up under the phone in flow, and the hold is sized so the
   * copy starts 40% of the phone's height below the phone's top. `.pin`'s bottom padding puts
   * the section's end 60% down the phone at release, and the section clips what overhangs it.
   * (`CLAUDE.md`'s warning about negative bottom margins on sticky layers is this effect, used
   * on purpose here.)
   *
   * After the reduced-motion block on purpose, so it wins at this width in both modes: the
   * reduced frame's absolute `top`s belong to the pinned composition this width no longer
   * has. Under reduced motion `notify.ts` rests the screen lit.
   */
  @media (max-width: 767px) {
    .notify {
      --release-gap: var(--spacing-16);
      /* Round 3: the section cuts the phone rather than ending under it. `clip`, never
       * `hidden`, for the sticky phone's sake. */
      overflow-y: clip;
    }

    /* The budget lives in `.hold` inside `.pin` now, not in spacers beside it. */
    .travel.sleep,
    .travel.wake,
    .travel.read,
    .travel.close,
    .travel.rest {
      height: 0;
    }

    .pin {
      position: relative;
      display: flex;
      flex-direction: column;
      height: auto;
      /* 0 since 8.14c round 7 (owner: closer to the playlist, whose own bottom padding is the
       * gap now). */
      padding-top: 0;
      padding-bottom: calc(0.6 * var(--device-h) + var(--release-gap));
      /* `clip` on x only, never `hidden`: a scroll container would un-pin the phone, and
       * `contain: paint` clips just as surely, so both are dropped. The halo is wider than
       * the screen, which is what the horizontal clip is for. */
      overflow: visible;
      overflow-x: clip;
      contain: none;
    }

    /* The frame-anchored cast light assumes a 100svh pin. The halo replaces it. */
    .cast {
      display: none;
    }

    /* After the beats, over the phone. Plain content: never faded, never moved by script. */
    .copy {
      order: 1;
      position: relative;
      left: auto;
      top: auto;
      bottom: auto;
      width: auto;
      padding-inline: var(--gutter);
      transform: none;
      opacity: 1;
      will-change: auto;
    }

    /* The section's own phone size, as it was before this width had a branch. Pinned with
     * 70% of it on screen. */
    .device-slot {
      position: sticky;
      top: calc(100svh - 0.7 * var(--device-h));
      left: auto;
      flex: none;
      height: var(--device-h);
      margin: 0 auto calc(-1 * (var(--device-h) + var(--release-gap)));
      transform: none;
    }

    /* `.cast`, centred on the phone it is cast by. Same light, same signal. */
    .halo {
      /* Bigger and brighter than `.cast` (round 4, owner: "barely visible on mobile"), then
       * brought back halfway at round 5: 170vw / 0.55 / an 88%-30% core before, 240vw / 1 /
       * 100%-50% at round 4, the middle of the two at round 5, and 20% smaller at round 6
       * with its brightness unchanged. */
      --halo-w: 164vw;
      display: block;
      position: absolute;
      left: 50%;
      top: 55%;
      z-index: -1;
      width: var(--halo-w);
      aspect-ratio: 1;
      transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
      pointer-events: none;
      background: radial-gradient(
        circle closest-side,
        color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-bloom-amber) 94%, transparent) 0%,
        color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-bloom-amber) 40%, transparent) 35%,
        transparent 69%
      );
      opacity: calc(var(--notify-wake) * 0.775);
      /*
       * Faded out above the section's cut (60% down the phone), so the light does not end on
       * the section's straight bottom edge. The halo's top edge is
       * `0.55 * --device-h - --halo-w / 2` in the phone's coordinates, hence the offsets.
       */
      -webkit-mask-image: linear-gradient(
        to bottom,
        #000 calc(var(--halo-w) / 2 - 0.25 * var(--device-h)),
        transparent calc(var(--halo-w) / 2 + 0.05 * var(--device-h))
      );
      mask-image: linear-gradient(
        to bottom,
        #000 calc(var(--halo-w) / 2 - 0.25 * var(--device-h)),
        transparent calc(var(--halo-w) / 2 + 0.05 * var(--device-h))
      );
    }

    /*
     * The asleep screen starts half lit rather than black (8.14c round 6, owner: "make the
     * phone mockup start from a higher opacity"). `LockScreen`'s `.off` layer is shared with
     * desktop's frozen act, so it is overridden here, anchored under this section's own
     * `.device-slot`, and still reaches 0 at the wake's peak.
     */
    .device-slot :global(.off) {
      opacity: calc(0.5 * (1 - var(--notify-wake)));
    }

    /* Puts the copy's top 40% of the phone's height below the phone's top, once the phone's
     * negative margin has pulled it up: the copy enters the screen with 40% of the phone in view. */
    .hold {
      display: block;
      flex: none;
      height: calc(0.4 * var(--device-h) + var(--release-gap));
    }

    .cta {
      display: none;
    }
  }
</style>
